CLOS condition classes are defined with (STANDARD-OBJECT) parents instead of their
standard CL superclasses, so TYPEP against an ancestor fails.

STATUS: open.  Found 2026-06-25 while loading ASDF/UIOP (it WARNs a STYLE-WARNING
subclass, which bocl's strict WARN rejects because the class is not TYPEP 'WARNING).

== Symptom ==
    (typep (make-condition 'style-warning) 'warning)         => NIL   ; expected T
    (warn 'asdf::define-package-style-warning ...)            => TYPE-ERROR
        "WARN: #<DEFINE-PACKAGE-STYLE-WARNING> is not of type WARNING"
ASDF's (define-condition define-package-style-warning (style-warning) ...) is a
WARNING subtype, but bocl breaks the chain at STYLE-WARNING.

== Cause ==
bootstrap/prelude.lisp registers the condition hierarchy TWICE:
1. An ENSURE-CLOS-CLASS dolist (~line 4929) with the CORRECT parents:
     (style-warning warning) (storage-condition serious-condition)
     (error serious-condition) ... etc.
2. A later block of DEFCLASS forms (~lines 5406-5429) that RE-define a subset with
   (STANDARD-OBJECT) as the only superclass, clobbering the correct parents:
     (defclass style-warning      (standard-object) ())   ; should be (warning)
     (defclass storage-condition  (standard-object) ())   ; should be (serious-condition)
     (defclass program-error      (standard-object) ())   ; should be (error)
     (defclass control-error      (standard-object) ())   ; should be (error)
     (defclass cell-error         (standard-object) (...)) ; should be (error)
     (defclass arithmetic-error   (standard-object) (...)) ; should be (error)
     (defclass stream-error       (standard-object) (...)) ; should be (error)
     (defclass file-error         (standard-object) (...)) ; should be (error)
     (defclass parse-error        (standard-object) ())    ; should be (error)
     (defclass print-not-readable (standard-object) (...)) ; should be (error)
So these stand-alone classes are disconnected from CONDITION/SERIOUS-CONDITION/
ERROR/WARNING, and TYPEP / handler dispatch on an ancestor misses them.

== Fix ==
Give each of those DEFCLASS forms its proper CL superclass (matching the dolist).
The comment there worried about "shadowing kernel classes", but inheriting FROM
error/warning (resolved to the CLOS classes the dolist already registered) is exactly
what is needed; HANDLER-CASE looks up kernel classes first, so kernel-condition
dispatch is unaffected.  Re-run must-condition (147/0) and the must-* suite after the
change.

== Relevance ==
Blocks ASDF/UIOP loading at the first WARN.  Also a plain conformance bug: e.g.
(handler-case (error 'program-error ...) (error () :caught)) should catch via the
ERROR ancestor.  Note: bocl's WARN intentionally type-checks its condition (issue
must-condition forms 19-24) — the WARN check is correct; the class hierarchy is what
is wrong.
